Q: Using &quot;this-&gt;member_variable&quot; as template function parameter A: <p>Maybe a simple solution is to provide the instance of  <code>Faewe</code> as argument of the <code>LOG</code> constructor:</p>
<pre><code>struct LOG {
    template &lt;typename T, typename... Ts&gt;
    LOG (T const&amp; obj, const char * charPtr, Ts &amp;&amp;... LogArguments)  {
        std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;In LOG: m_member=&quot; &lt;&lt; obj.m_member &lt;&lt; std::endl;
    }
};
</code></pre>
<p>then you create a member <code>Faewe::log</code> as follows:</p>
<pre><code>template&lt;typename...Ts&gt;
auto log(Ts&amp;&amp;...ts) { 
  LOG(*this, std::forward&lt;Ts&gt;(ts)...); 
}
</code></pre>
<p>that will create the <code>LOG</code> instance by providing itself as first argument.</p>
<p>The whole example would be:</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;

struct LOG {
    template &lt;typename T, typename... Ts&gt;
    LOG (T const&amp; obj, const char * charPtr, Ts &amp;&amp;... LogArguments)  {
        std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;In LOG: m_member=&quot; &lt;&lt; obj.m_member &lt;&lt; std::endl;
    }
};

struct Faewe {
    template&lt;typename...Ts&gt;
    auto log(Ts&amp;&amp;...ts) {
        LOG(*this, std::forward&lt;Ts&gt;(ts)...);
    }

    int m_member {73};
    auto doSomething() -&gt; void {
        log(&quot;hello&quot;, 73);
    }
};

int main()
{
    Faewe faewe;
    faewe.doSomething();
    return 0;
}
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://godbolt.org/z/boK644c44" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Demo</a></p>
<p>The CRTP's advantage of <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/79875558/2229039">this answer</a> is that it is somewhat less intrusive for <code>Faewe</code>.</p>
<p>From a design point of view, the CRTP's approach &quot;includes&quot; the log feature into <code>Faewe</code> and in this answer <code>Faewe</code> simply &quot;uses&quot; the log feature.</p>

Q: UI Freezing when passing prop function from parent to child component A: <p>Your issue is caused by unnecessary re-renders of the parent component, which recreate the callback function on every render. This forces <code>MaterialReactTable</code> to reinitialize repeatedly and causes the UI to freeze after the first click.</p>
<p>In order to fix this you should:</p>
<ul>
<li>Memoize the callback in the parent using <code>useCallback</code></li>
<li>Memoize the child component using <code>React.memo</code></li>
</ul>
<p>Below is simplified setup of your components:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const Support = () =&gt; {
  const [message, setMessage] = useState(null);

  const handleChildData = useCallback((data) =&gt; {
    setMessage(data);
  }, []);

  return (
    &lt;&gt;
      &lt;h1&gt;{message} : Data from Child&lt;/h1&gt;
      &lt;GridData handleChildData={handleChildData} /&gt;
    &lt;/&gt;
  );
};

const GridData = React.memo(({ handleChildData }) =&gt; {
  const table = useMaterialReactTable(
    useMemo(() =&gt; ({
      data,
      columns,
      muiTableBodyRowProps: ({ row }) =&gt; ({
        onClick: () =&gt; handleChildData(row.original.name),
        sx: { cursor: 'pointer' },
      }),
    }), [data, columns, handleChildData])
  );

  return &lt;MaterialReactTable table={table} /&gt;;
});
</code></pre>
<p><strong>BONUS</strong></p>
<p>Setters returned from <code>useState</code> hook are stable, so you can pass <code>setMesage</code> function and omit entirely</p>
<pre><code>const handleChildData = useCallback((data) =&gt; {
    setMessage(data);
}, []);
</code></pre>
<p>and then pass <code>setMessage</code> directly</p>
<pre><code>&lt;GridData handleChildData={setMessage} /&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>It will keep working the same :)</p>
